---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import StaticCode from "../../components/StaticCode.astro";
import { site } from "../../lib/site";

const toc = [
  { id: "prerequisites", label: "Prerequisites" },
  { id: "build", label: "Build" },
  { id: "layout", label: "Project layout" },
  { id: "first", label: "First program" },
  { id: "targets", label: "Generate a target" },
  { id: "editor", label: "VS Code extension" },
  { id: "verify", label: "Verify everything" },
  { id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting" },
  { id: "exit-codes", label: "Exit codes" },
];

const install = `npm install
npm run build
npm link

hs doctor`;
const first = `// examples/calculate.hs
fn calculate(x: i32, bonus: bool) -> i32 {
    let base: i32 = x * 3;

    if bonus {
        return base + 2;
    }

    return base;
}`;
const commands = `hs check examples/calculate.hs

hs build examples/calculate.hs --target rust --entry calculate

hs build examples/calculate.hs --target csharp --entry calculate --run --args 10,true
hs build examples/calculate.hs --target cpp --entry calculate --run --args 10,true
hs build examples/calculate.hs --target javascript --entry calculate --run --args 10,true`;
const editor = `npm run vscode:install`;
const verify = `npm test

# or the conformance suite alone
hs test --all-targets`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Getting started"
  description="Install the compiler, run the specification example, generate native projects, and install the editor extension."
  active="/docs/getting-started"
  toc={toc}
  prev={{ href: "/docs", label: "Introduction" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/language", label: "Language core" }}
>
  <h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>
  <p>
    h# {site.version} was verified with Node.js 24.21, TypeScript 7.0.2, .NET SDK 10.0.401, Rust
    1.98, Go 1.27, OpenJDK 21, and g++ 13.2. The CLI, compiler, and JavaScript target run on Node 22 or later.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>Required command</th><th>Generated project</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>C#</td><td><code>dotnet</code> 10+</td><td><code>net10.0</code> console project</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rust</td><td><code>cargo</code>, <code>rustc</code></td><td>Cargo binary, edition 2021</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Go</td><td><code>go</code> 1.22+</td><td><code>go.mod</code> + <code>main.go</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Java</td><td><code>javac</code>, <code>java</code></td><td><code>HsProgram.java</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>C++</td><td><code>g++</code> or <code>clang++</code></td><td>C++20 <code>main.cpp</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>JavaScript</td><td><code>node</code> 22+</td><td><code>main.js</code> + <code>package.json</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Missing toolchains do not break compilation. They mark the corresponding verification target
    <strong>blocked</strong>, never passing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="build">Build</h2>
  <StaticCode code={install} language="bash" title="terminal" meta="setup" />
  <p>
    <code>npm link</code> installs the <code>hs</code>, <code>hsharp</code>, and <code>hcharp</code>
    commands. <code>hs doctor</code> prints the detected toolchain versions and writes
    <code>reports/environment.json</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="layout">Project layout</h2>
  <p>The parts you will touch while following this guide:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>src/</code></td><td>Compiler, checker, interpreter, generators, CLI, and bridge.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>examples/</code></td><td>Conformance programs, including the calculate example.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>profiles/</code></td><td>Standard and personal profile JSON documents.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>build/</code></td><td>Generated target projects, created by the build command.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>local/</code></td><td>Export/import scratch files and the demo workspace.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>reports/</code></td><td>Doctor and conformance evidence.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>editors/vscode/</code></td><td>Bundled Visual Studio Code extension.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Build, local, and reports are generated. Profiles, examples, and the specification are inputs;
    generated output can be deleted and recreated.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first">First program</h2>
  <StaticCode code={first} language="hsharp" title="examples/calculate.hs" meta="standard profile" />
  <p>
    Run <code>hs check examples/calculate.hs</code>. The checker reports syntax, names, types,
    missing returns, and call-graph cycles with <code>file:line:column</code> locations. A successful
    check is not an execution; use <code>--run</code> or the conformance harness for outcomes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>calculate(10, true)</code> returns <code>32</code>. The maximum i32 plus one returns
    <code>E_OVERFLOW</code>; <code>false &amp;&amp; ((x + 1) &gt; 0)</code> returns <code>false</code>
    without evaluating the right side.
  </p>

  <h2 id="targets">Generate a target</h2>
  <StaticCode code={commands} language="bash" title="terminal" meta="check and build" />
  <p>
    Generated projects are written under <code>build/&lt;program&gt;/&lt;target&gt;/</code> by
    default. They are ordinary projects; the target's own tools compile them. <code>--run</code>
    compiles and executes the entry function, printing one JSON outcome line.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use <code>--profile &lt;file.json&gt;</code> when the source is written in a personal notation.
    Canonical source uses the standard profile and is never interpreted through an implicit local
    preference.
  </p>

  <h2 id="editor">VS Code extension</h2>
  <StaticCode code={editor} language="bash" title="terminal" meta="bundled compiler" />
  <p>
    The extension bundles the compiler into the extension host: no external <code>hs</code> process
    is needed for diagnostics or preview. Open a <code>.hs</code> file and run
    <strong>h#: Show Generated Code</strong> from the command palette.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read the <a href="/vscode">full VS Code extension guide</a> for installation, commands, settings,
    and current limitations.
  </p>

  <h2 id="verify">Verify everything</h2>
  <StaticCode code={verify} language="bash" title="terminal" meta="full suite" />
  <p>
    <code>npm test</code> runs 57 unit tests, the VS Code typecheck, the bridge protocol test, and
    the full conformance suite. <code>hs test --all-targets</code> writes
    <code>reports/conformance.json</code> and <code>reports/conformance.md</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>The hs command is not recognized</td><td>Run npm link from the repository root, or invoke node dist/cli/index.js.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A target is marked blocked</td><td>Run hs doctor, install the missing toolchain, then rerun the command.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A profile cannot be found</td><td>Pass an explicit profile path; personal profiles are never selected implicitly.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Unexpected E_OVERFLOW</td><td>The operation left the i32 range. Checked arithmetic never wraps or clamps.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The wrong function runs</td><td>Pass an explicit entry function. Inference only accepts main or a single function.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="exit-codes">Exit codes</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>All executed checks passed, or the command succeeded</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>Program/check failure, build failure, or failed case</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>Usage error, or at least one target blocked</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</DocsLayout>
